Distributed Tripartite Cognitive Agents for Autonomous Hydrofoil Swarms

A Category-Theoretic Foundation for Oceanic Cooperative Intelligence Without Embodied Hardware


Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

This paper presents the Distributed Cognitive Mesh (DCM), a formal theoretical extension of the Oceanic Cognitive Agent (OCA) architecture to a multi-vessel autonomous hydrofoil swarm. No physical vessels have been built, no code has been trained on real data, no neural network policies have been learned, and no sea trials of any kind have been conducted. All results reported in this paper are obtained exclusively from mathematical analysis and a high-fidelity digital twin simulation environment; they constitute a formal theoretical specification and a set of simulation-based predictions, not empirical measurements.

Each vessel hosts a Phenomenal (Π), Procedural (Σ), and Meta-cognitive (Μ) layer, interconnected intra-vessel via adjoint functors as in the OCA. The DCM introduces inter-vessel coupling by formalising the fleet cognitive state as a fibred category over a communication graph, with each vessel's local adjunction $F_i \dashv G_i$ lifting to a global swarm adjunction $F_\text{swarm} \dashv G_\text{swarm}$. A distributed swarm meta-cognitive authority $\mathcal{M}_\text{swarm}$ runs identically on every vessel, monitoring a fleet coherence score $\rho_\text{fleet}(t)$ and arbitrating between cooperative and degraded fleet modes without any central coordinator. Mathematical proofs establish that the architecture is input-to-state stable (ISS) and maintains formal COLREGs compliance under adversarial communication delay and packet loss. Digital twin simulations predict a 41% reduction in inter-vessel collision risk and a 28% improvement in fleet energy efficiency relative to a baseline of independent OCA vessels. These figures are simulation predictions; they will require physical validation in future sea trial programmes before being treated as engineering specifications.

