## 1. Introduction

A single autonomous hydrofoil equipped with the Oceanic Cognitive Agent (Maakoul, 2026) reasons about its own uncertainty, models its rider's intent, and gracefully escalates control authority when its deliberative layer becomes unreliable. Many maritime operations — port logistics, search-and-rescue, environmental monitoring — demand coordinated action by multiple vessels. Extending the OCA to a swarm requires more than adding a communication channel; it requires a cognitive architecture that treats the fleet itself as an epistemic agent, capable of forming collective beliefs, arbitrating between conflicting local intentions, and degrading gracefully when inter-vessel links fail.

Existing multi-agent reinforcement learning and centralised model-predictive control approaches fail to provide hard safety guarantees and are brittle under the partial observability and communication dropout typical of the ocean. The DCM adopts a different philosophy: delegate deliberative planning entirely to each vessel's existing OCA stack, then overlay a mathematically defined coupling structure that drives the fleet toward a coherent, safe, and energy-efficient global behaviour without a single point of failure.

This paper is a purely theoretical and simulation-based contribution. No hardware has been fabricated for this work. No policies have been trained on physical sensor data. No vessel has been tested on water. The Π layer reasoning engine uses symbolic constrained optimisation and Bayesian filtering — analytically specified, not learned from experience. The Σ layer relies on the analytically derived hydrodynamic NMPC from the v4.0 hydrofoil specification. The Μ layer uses closed-form entropy estimators and the adjunction coherence metric. All claims about performance are digital twin predictions that will need to be validated in hardware-in-the-loop tests and incremental sea trials before any engineering conclusion can be drawn. The contribution of this paper is a formal mathematical architecture and a simulation-based feasibility demonstration, not an empirical result.

The paper is organised as follows. Section 2 reviews the single-vessel OCA and defines the multi-vessel problem. Section 3 develops the category-theoretic formalisation of the swarm. Section 4 describes the digital twin integration. Section 5 presents the extended fleet cost functional and formal safety proofs. Section 6 reports simulation predictions. Section 7 discusses what physical implementation would require. Section 8 concludes.

