## 3. Category-Theoretic Formalisation of the Swarm

### 3.1 Cognitive Fibred Category

Let $\mathbf{B}$ be the base category whose objects are vessel indices $i \in \{1,\dots,N\}$ and whose morphisms are communication links $i \to j$ whenever $(i,j) \in \mathcal{E}$. For each vessel $i$, the local cognitive category $\mathbf{C}_i$ is the product of its $\mathbf{C}_{\Pi,i}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{\Sigma,i}$, coupled by the adjunction $F_i \dashv G_i$. The assignment $i \mapsto \mathbf{C}_i$, together with functors between fibres for each graph edge, defines a **fibred category** $\mathcal{F}$ over $\mathbf{B}$.

For each communication link $(i,j) \in \mathcal{E}$, we define a **belief-transfer functor**:

$$T_{ij} : \mathbf{C}_{\Pi,i} \to \mathbf{C}_{\Pi,j}$$

that transports the cognitive token from vessel $i$ to vessel $j$. Concretely, $T_{ij}(\pi_i)$ is the Bayesian belief update that vessel $j$'s Π layer performs upon receiving vessel $i$'s token. $T_{ij}$ is stipulated to be a left adjoint to the reverse transfer functor $T_{ji}$, reflecting the symmetry of bidirectional information exchange.

### 3.2 Global Adjunction and Fleet Coherence

The fibration structure supports the definition of a global cognitive category $\mathbf{C}_\text{swarm}$ as the category of **descent data** for $\mathcal{F}$: a swarm cognitive state is a family $\{\pi_i\}_{i=1}^N$ such that each $\pi_i$ is compatible with the belief transported from every neighbour $j$, i.e., $\pi_i \approx T_{ji}(\pi_j)$ for all $(j,i) \in \mathcal{E}$. The swarm-level Phenomenal category and the swarm-level Procedural category similarly emerge from the descent data of the respective fibrations, yielding a **global adjunction**:

$$F_\text{swarm} \dashv G_\text{swarm}$$

between $\mathbf{C}_{\Pi,\text{swarm}}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{\Sigma,\text{swarm}}$. The **fleet coherence score** is then defined as the normalised counit residual across all vessels:

$$\rho_\text{fleet}(t) = 1 - \frac{\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^N \bigl\|\varepsilon_{\sigma_i(t)} - \sigma_i(t)\bigr\|}{\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^N \bigl\|\sigma_i(t)\bigr\|}$$

When $\rho_\text{fleet} \approx 1$, the collective intent of the fleet is faithfully executed by all procedural layers. When $\rho_\text{fleet}$ falls, the swarm-wide meta-cognitive authority intervenes.

### 3.3 Swarm-Wide Meta-Cognitive Authority $\mathcal{M}_\text{swarm}}$

Each vessel runs an identical instance of $\mathcal{M}_\text{swarm}$, which computes $\rho_\text{fleet}$ from received tokens. The authority protocol defines four fleet modes:

| Fleet Mode | Condition | Swarm Behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| Coherent | $\rho_\text{fleet} > \theta_\text{fleet}$ | Full coordination via token exchange; global intent optimised |
| Disjoint | Some $H_{\Pi,i} > \theta_\Pi$ or $\rho_i < \rho_\min$ | Affected vessels enter Mode 2; neighbours treat them as dynamic obstacles with enlarged safety margins |
| Partitioned | Communication graph partitioned | Each connected component runs independent DCM; reconciliation on reconnection via Lamport-timestamped Last-Writer-Wins merge |
| Emergency | $\rho_\text{fleet} < \theta_\text{crit}$ or any vessel in Mode 3 | All vessels enter Mode 3 (Reflex Handoff), dispersing to pre-defined safe holding points |

Mode 3 is a per-vessel hardware guarantee. Even if every other vessel in the swarm computes a setpoint that would require a reckless manoeuvre from vessel $i$, the physically isolated Reflex layer on vessel $i$ will override it. The DCM has no mechanism to circumvent this: its authority is limited strictly to restricting setpoint bounds, never expanding them.

