## 7. What Physical Implementation Would Require

The DCM as presented is a mathematical specification and simulation-based feasibility study. Translating it to operational vessels would require, at minimum, the following stages — none of which have been undertaken:

**Stage 1 — Hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) testing.** The Reflex and Σ layers would be run on physical embedded hardware (ARM Cortex-R5 and Cortex-A78 targets) connected to a real-time simulator. This would validate computational latency claims (18 ms NMPC solve time, 0.5 ms Μ layer) against actual processor performance.

**Stage 2 — Single-vessel sea trials.** The single-vessel OCA must be validated on water before any multi-vessel deployment. This would establish whether the EKF state estimator, wave-field predictor, and NMPC assumptions hold in real sea conditions.

**Stage 3 — Communication channel characterisation.** The simulated link model (100–500 ms latency, 0–30% loss) must be validated against measured maritime VHF and LTE performance in the intended operational area. Token robustness under real electromagnetic interference must be tested.

**Stage 4 — Two-vessel coordinated trials.** The cognitive token exchange and belief propagation protocol must be validated for two vessels before scaling to a fleet.

**Stage 5 — Fleet sea trials.** Multi-vessel coordination, COLREGs compliance, and emergency dispersion must be validated incrementally, beginning in controlled conditions and expanding the operational envelope.

The results in Section 6 provide the theoretical motivation for undertaking this programme, not a substitute for it.

---

## 8. Conclusion

The Distributed Cognitive Mesh formalises the extension of humanoid-style tripartite cognition to a fleet of autonomous hydrofoils, using fibred categories, global adjunctions, and a distributed meta-cognitive authority to achieve coordinated intelligence without central control. The architecture is proven ISS and collision-free under stated assumptions. Digital twin simulations predict meaningful improvements in safety, energy efficiency, and fault tolerance over independent OCA operation.

The fundamental contribution of this paper is mathematical and architectural: a rigorous formal foundation for swarm cognitive coordination that generalises beyond hydrofoils to any distributed cyber-physical system requiring coordinated deliberative reasoning under uncertainty. No claim in this paper rests on physical experiment, trained policy, or sea trial. All performance figures are theoretical predictions subject to validation. The next step is a structured physical implementation programme, beginning with single-vessel hardware-in-the-loop testing and progressing incrementally to fleet sea trials.

