## Appendix A: Fibred Category Construction — Detailed Development

### A.1 The Base Category B

The base category **B** is defined as follows. Its objects are the vessel indices $\{1, \dots, N\}$. A morphism $i \to j$ exists if and only if $(i,j) \in \mathcal{E}$, the edge set of the communication graph $\mathcal{G}$. The identity morphism on each object is the self-loop; composition of morphisms $i \to j$ and $j \to k$ is the path $i \to k$, which exists only if the induced edge $(i,k)$ is also in $\mathcal{E}$ (i.e., composition is restricted to direct neighbours). This makes **B** a thin category — at most one morphism between any two objects — determined entirely by the adjacency structure of $\mathcal{G}$.

We note that **B** is not in general a groupoid: communication links may be directional (e.g., one vessel has higher transmit power), so the morphism $i \to j$ need not imply $j \to i$. In the DCM we assume bidirectional links for simplicity; the extension to directed graphs is straightforward.

### A.2 The Fibre Over Each Vessel

For each vessel $i$, the fibre $\mathbf{C}_i$ over the object $i \in \mathbf{B}$ is the local cognitive category defined in the OCA paper. Formally:

$$\mathbf{C}_i = \mathbf{C}_{\Pi,i} \times_{\mathbf{C}_{\Sigma,i}} \mathbf{C}_{\Sigma,i}$$

where the product is taken over the adjunction $F_i \dashv G_i$. The objects of $\mathbf{C}_i$ are pairs $(\pi_i, \sigma_i) \in C_{\Pi,i} \times C_{\Sigma,i}$ such that $\sigma_i$ lies in the image of $F_i(\pi_i)$ up to the coherence tolerance $1 - \rho_\min$. The morphisms of $\mathbf{C}_i$ are pairs of morphisms $(\phi_\Pi, \phi_\Sigma)$ in $\mathbf{C}_{\Pi,i}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{\Sigma,i}$ that commute with the adjunction unit $\eta_i$ and counit $\varepsilon_i$.

### A.3 The Belief-Transfer Functors

For each communication link $(i,j) \in \mathcal{E}$, the belief-transfer functor $T_{ij}: \mathbf{C}_{\Pi,i} \to \mathbf{C}_{\Pi,j}$ is defined on objects by:

$$T_{ij}(\pi_i) = \pi_j \oplus \Delta_{ij}(\pi_i)$$

where $\pi_j$ is vessel $j$'s current belief state and $\Delta_{ij}(\pi_i)$ is the Bayesian belief update induced by receiving vessel $i$'s cognitive token. Explicitly, if $\pi_i = (\mathcal{M}_i, \mathcal{I}_i, \mathcal{E}_i, \mathcal{B}_i)$ and the token encodes a summary statistic $s_i = (H_{\Pi,i}, r_{\Pi,i}^*, \rho_i)$, then:

$$\Delta_{ij}(\pi_i): \quad \mathcal{B}_j \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_j \cdot p(s_i \mid \mathcal{B}_j)$$

normalised to a valid distribution. On morphisms, $T_{ij}$ maps a deliberative transition $\pi_i \to \pi_i'$ (a belief update at vessel $i$) to the induced update $T_{ij}(\pi_i) \to T_{ij}(\pi_i')$ at vessel $j$, obtained by applying the same likelihood factor to $j$'s updated belief. Functoriality holds by the chain rule of probability.

### A.4 The Fibration Condition

The assignment $i \mapsto \mathbf{C}_i$ together with the functors $T_{ij}$ satisfies the fibration condition if for every composable pair of links $(i \to j)$ and $(j \to k)$ there exists a natural isomorphism:

$$T_{ik} \cong T_{jk} \circ T_{ij}$$

This holds in our setting because the Bayesian belief update is associative: receiving the token from $i$ at $j$, then $j$ forwarding a derived token to $k$, produces the same belief update at $k$ as receiving $i$'s token directly, up to the information loss inherent in token summarisation. We formalise this as a pseudo-functor (rather than a strict functor) from **B** to **Cat** (the category of categories), where the natural isomorphisms $T_{ik} \cong T_{jk} \circ T_{ij}$ are the structure 2-cells. The fibration is therefore a 2-fibration over **B** in the sense of Hermida (1999), accounting for the non-strictness introduced by the token summarisation.

### A.5 Descent Data and the Global Swarm Category

A **descent datum** for the fibration $\mathcal{F}$ is a family $\{\pi_i\}_{i=1}^N$ of cognitive states — one per vessel — together with coherence isomorphisms $\phi_{ij}: T_{ij}(\pi_i) \xrightarrow{\sim} \pi_j$ for all $(i,j) \in \mathcal{E}$, satisfying the cocycle condition:

$$\phi_{jk} \circ T_{jk}(\phi_{ij}) = \phi_{ik} \qquad \forall\, (i,j), (j,k) \in \mathcal{E}$$

The global swarm cognitive category $\mathbf{C}_\text{swarm}$ is defined as the category of descent data: its objects are consistent families $\{(\pi_i, \phi_{ij})\}$ and its morphisms are families of local morphisms that respect the coherence isomorphisms. Intuitively, an object of $\mathbf{C}_\text{swarm}$ is a fleet-wide belief state in which every vessel's world model is mutually consistent with every neighbour's model, up to the approximation inherent in token exchange.

When $\mathcal{G}$ is connected and all tokens arrive without loss, descent data are equivalent to a single global belief state shared by all vessels — the fleet converges to a common world model. When the graph is partitioned, descent data decompose into independent families over each connected component, matching the Partitioned fleet mode of Section 3.3.

