## Appendix F: Digital Twin Simulation Protocol — Full Specification

This appendix documents the complete simulation methodology underlying the results reported in Section 6. The purpose is to ensure reproducibility within the digital twin environment and to make the assumptions and limitations of the simulation explicit.

### F.1 Sea State Generation

Wave fields are generated using the JONSWAP spectrum:

$$S(\omega) = \frac{\alpha g^2}{\omega^5} \exp\!\left[-\frac{5}{4}\left(\frac{\omega_p}{\omega}\right)^4\right] \gamma^{\exp\!\left[-(\omega - \omega_p)^2 / (2\sigma^2 \omega_p^2)\right]}$$

with significant wave height $H_s \in [0.2, 2.5]$ m, peak period $T_p \in [3, 15]$ s, and peak enhancement factor $\gamma \in [1.5, 3.5]$, sampled uniformly at random for each mission. Directional spreading uses a $\cos^2$ distribution. Wind gust forcing is modelled as an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process with mean wind speed $\bar{U}_w \in [0, 12]$ m/s and correlation time 30 s. Wave-field realisations are pre-computed and stored; each of the 10,000 missions uses a unique realisation.

### F.2 Vessel and Fleet Configuration

For each mission, the number of vessels $N$ is drawn uniformly from $\{3, \dots, 15\}$. Initial positions are drawn uniformly within a $2 \times 2$ km operational area with minimum initial separation $3 d_\min$. Vessel mass, foil geometry, and battery state are drawn from distributions reflecting manufacturing tolerances (mass ±5%, foil area ±2%, SOH 80–100%). Each vessel runs the theoretically specified OCA stack; no trained neural network policies are used at any stage.

### F.3 Communication Channel Model

The communication channel between each pair of vessels is modelled as a Gilbert-Elliott two-state Markov chain, with a Good state (low loss, low latency) and a Bad state (high loss, high latency). Transition probabilities are calibrated to match published VHF maritime channel measurements (Parsons et al., 2019, used as a reference for parameter selection; the simulation does not reproduce the exact experimental conditions of that study). Link parameters:

| Parameter | Good state | Bad state |
|---|---|---|
| Packet loss probability | 2% | 35% |
| Latency | 80–150 ms | 300–600 ms |
| State sojourn time | Exp(mean 120 s) | Exp(mean 15 s) |

Bandwidth is not explicitly modelled; the 168-byte token at 1 Hz per vessel is assumed to fit within available bandwidth for all fleet sizes considered.

### F.4 Fault Injection Protocol

Each mission includes a randomised subset of the following fault events:

- GPS dropout: vessel $i$ loses GNSS for a duration drawn from Exp(mean 45 s), at a random time in the mission. Frequency: 0–2 events per vessel per mission.
- Communication partition: the graph $\mathcal{G}$ is split into two random connected components for a duration drawn from Exp(mean 300 s). Frequency: 0–1 events per mission.
- Sensor noise spike: IMU noise standard deviation increases by a factor of 10 for 5 s, simulating a wave slamming event. Frequency: 0–5 events per vessel per mission.
- Adversarial rider input: rider weight-transfer vector $\tau_\text{rider}$ follows an erratic random walk for 30 s, simulating an inexperienced rider in rough conditions. Frequency: 0–3 events per mission.

### F.5 Metrics and Statistical Analysis

Each metric is computed over 10,000 missions. The reported figures are means; 95% confidence intervals are computed by bootstrap resampling (1,000 resamples). The confidence intervals are not reported in the main body for readability; full distributions are available in the supplementary simulation data. The improvement percentages reported in Table 1 of Section 6 are statistically significant at the $p < 0.01$ level under a two-sided Welch's t-test for the independent-samples comparison of DCM versus independent OCA. This statistical significance applies only within the simulation environment and does not imply physical significance.

